ght corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}?$" :question/worked-solution "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "691136a3-c8a5-53ad-8f6d-c6b62b26d2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386897} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1ff36f-0d8d-575f-a2e6-11d23df75e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386898} {:answer/id #uuid "de319e43-ff4a-5fb9-bb56-990836c287f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386899} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc895f-c91f-5a56-9fed-858ae28ad8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386900} {:answer/id #uuid "c662477e-f2ce-5416-aaa2-7348143c6498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "691136a3-c8a5-53ad-8f6d-c6b62b26d2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386897} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e5e3905-c2b1-520e-a32d-31b10c414d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4ff74f6-4836-56a3-8d9f-d719958e2599" :question/math-academy-id "q-11876" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a240b7c7ad19758ceaa9c2d6b90cb8308f7bd05c2e6e318bb6654ddf743d0136.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/b6/b6394c3b265b4c49fc6e7d1284ed63c176216f755824578e6bf2d9ef4de8b2bb.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(2,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f570287d-b0e4-53bb-8fa1-37348ca9202f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Differential Equation Using Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1101527-9f4f-5301-a5d7-2c4675ea434d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)={e}^{x-3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386904} {:answer/id #uuid "702773f0-c93f-5fdc-8cfc-c4f417f927cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386905} {:answer/id #uuid "db2dc2fe-2a07-5f7b-a570-4e58c725722c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=3{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386906} {:answer/id #uuid "09cf98b5-cc32-56bd-a7bc-80017c397c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)={e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386907} {:answer/id #uuid "2723b2b5-87a6-52a2-bd3a-988db461edac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=-{e}^{-x/3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db2dc2fe-2a07-5f7b-a570-4e58c725722c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=3{e}^{-x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386906} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5995d24-fede-525d-a40c-73179ae79979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57dad966-30e8-503a-bfa2-6fa76bd6e7c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119033" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)+3{e}^{-x}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-3{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =-3\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-{e}^{-x})+C \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdbca57f-16aa-507a-9243-fc5de6668bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4acfee397d2c301aa7d005c8467319969bdfe1a487c284e51024a1851ef8a2e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386911} {:answer/id #uuid "0e615e5e-edb5-5cf5-9379-57b9b7c05caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e2d84423e017f6d126ef6b997a50d62a7de30f91c24aa64f2525ce4ae33cdd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386912} {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7a98-d892-56d4-877b-036894b8ae90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/146aa5565c845f3fbfa66e6c215224236869199844cdbe51f2671c781b1c3705.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386913} {:answer/id #uuid "35676c31-bad6-527d-8d41-0491046fbe5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e93190f38d95bd30749fbd1342812c93f593a1d78243f810a3822d5d30eff82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386914} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ce449-3435-5f49-bbc8-8c5d5d34005e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/557708220ba3876b0ae9dbf1d265d11e8d98cf1102ad084d93419543ede35c4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7a98-d892-56d4-877b-036894b8ae90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/146aa5565c845f3fbfa66e6c215224236869199844cdbe51f2671c781b1c3705.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386913} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd2a4ff-6ada-5a27-9174-b3c1a697aa04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06acaa38-08e2-563c-a31d-7a927dbf62a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119139" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc2ee25a85be3ffdb055853f7665bd53d34003a74b96a14bb947bed39b18e83f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641c6f0c-3eaf-561b-8356-41cd00f60ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5f4f2b65878b9c152b5c87e1fac0473f0e76455fc5762ba10f21555d68a60c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386918} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f2778-f343-5bd4-8b1f-ffa35692fd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e475d534a8f090caa9611d865e70a9717969ddc541c55d98200e19031bffe84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386919} {:answer/id #uuid "21fe63ba-ca2a-5062-b8c9-c489ae9d0da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a65cbd746c3bb9abe495af168fd7c63e83ecf3d942c5fb478f3d7d312d140b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386920} {:answer/id #uuid "993af450-e9a5-57fb-a473-cf3bc2a14c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95b3d8fc43562341f93a2bccbcbe36d2a070a2dd6687606175ab5e9580f86e2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386921} {:answer/id #uuid "6f16a9da-248f-5eb6-9d9c-67a9d932876b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0d14e369d621a6c2d06b4db1485ff43f6af4be2014e9d26564c9ca11e9bd38cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f2778-f343-5bd4-8b1f-ffa35692fd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e475d534a8f090caa9611d865e70a9717969ddc541c55d98200e19031bffe84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386919} :answer-field/id #uuid "33aa3810-dbfd-5996-9796-56ae579de721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a05048db-be50-5d0c-8e66-1eb6eda34260" :question/math-academy-id "q-119142" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\int (x-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/e0/e0ff2e64925dcd8bdf298c47815f6e2982fe6091e1ff5dea042f585c67ef1721.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2936c86-e02f-5b66-8fe2-5aa81aab8754" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21542c69-029a-5e5a-9ef9-f46126bb5513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5e9f55d20c0b7fc515ea598d150017148342a6ba9082068823dd23c01e0eede.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386925} {:answer/id #uuid "fc349a19-13c9-5968-89fc-5f6cdf460067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/911af9ca268f5f4c9cbac7b9c063eae54defce6d99dfadef609892b93ad58ae4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386926} {:answer/id #uuid "1f44a7e7-14a1-5abd-a1cd-add670d842bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d1215951efd9dbf2abcb8fe1c4a0856bbaa9bff70a3ae69c0da33bc2d157f82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386927} {:answer/id #uuid "845f8d4d-3d72-5f3e-823d-33923ee96ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d6fc3c9462967aec890b1f681460a7b350b580c1b5bb55d37ccfe88c485eb36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386928} {:answer/id #uuid "17783f88-9599-594b-9d64-74559758ed76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/467f0de9a886dbdb4d7620a4249379c2a8a661738960c3242d5e6618c35eb22b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc349a19-13c9-5968-89fc-5f6cdf460067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/911af9ca268f5f4c9cbac7b9c063eae54defce6d99dfadef609892b93ad58ae4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386926} :answer-field/id #uuid "9464e844-09ab-5321-a0f3-485764921a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4db1550d-68e2-55f5-aa48-87d9e6b19e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-119184" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-x?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,2),(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=1-(-2)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=1-(-2)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=1-2=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/91/911af9ca268f5f4c9cbac7b9c063eae54defce6d99dfadef609892b93ad58ae4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5177f277-29a6-5df6-96c5-6bf53ffd9c51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6abb4bc8-d92c-5e08-809a-cdbf60f0a137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/888366506191b5bc66301411b71c812b2687438ba8ba224e417122146637df97.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386932} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea51b6e-2096-5bb1-8e3c-733106bfb528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7bc3179b68147f289cc2e64f233e9c851b6b6e88f583eaaf0cb641a10efdccdb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386933} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8c782d-8944-5929-b8a1-f44f33cdb56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a80441017c1733094d2b41707cb2a70ec6602148779c47db03684b1b89aa2c11.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386934} {:answer/id #uuid "0d956e53-1172-5037-bb66-8d2693409dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/07e3518d999acbf0b16aa97b1281853e5646cbd01aee9d3c452b646e619f0dde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386935} {:answer/id #uuid "5e21e02c-d193-58c6-be76-843731635b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6e/6e4fed6350e22f826f10c72b735896e00248d3799d226bdc6054961e5c772bc0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea51b6e-2096-5bb1-8e3c-733106bfb528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7bc3179b68147f289cc2e64f233e9c851b6b6e88f583eaaf0cb641a10efdccdb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386933} :answer-field/id #uuid "506bd37f-b401-5a59-93cf-eed2ec45db0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91ca0cb5-35a7-5d5c-9480-2128a21e6186" :question/math-academy-id "q-119237" :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope field does not vary with $y.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7bc3179b68147f289cc2e64f233e9c851b6b6e88f583eaaf0cb641a10efdccdb.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go up and down the line. \n\n![](images/97/97f075f29a703d1cae44a533d4540093644d1fb19b7808feec6583d140f78de4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5177f277-29a6-5df6-96c5-6bf53ffd9c51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0eecf8-fcb8-52e8-bc16-b752fe62651c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/4746fd4a7c793882f09065278ad9a7350c41f91fcd35677e9ee8768a8ca57b66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386939} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6eca56-461c-5a1a-9d8f-849e9ed39cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7fa15a5bb016272df819a954be52a984a18d242d5617a5a02343b356ed38789c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386940} {:answer/id #uuid "92419e46-819d-5884-9a67-951c769dfaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/2666b0dbfa5cc6f725d2d656b968b46f2c0b344247985fd4859be36a8cef211e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386941} {:answer/id #uuid "8614eb80-7bca-5fda-bb06-0223693cbbb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebcc2639206da90f50f226b568538b8c20e6e29ecb0a9c44211331ffd1bce1b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386942} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4d0002-4317-57aa-abb9-584bea5a794e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e00df97d8362b857f927d3c572cf09a6e442ff1af639e7cfccaffdded5c4924d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4d0002-4317-57aa-abb9-584bea5a794e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e00df97d8362b857f927d3c572cf09a6e442ff1af639e7cfccaffdded5c4924d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386943} :answer-field/id #uuid "801e4846-e3a8-5b09-9eb7-dec602522839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcadf918-9522-5f15-827a-bb83d6e8c600" :question/math-academy-id "q-119239" :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope field does not vary with $y.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n![](images/e0/e00df97d8362b857f927d3c572cf09a6e442ff1af639e7cfccaffdded5c4924d.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go up and down the line. \n\n![](images/42/42e282935d15a2d48c07a25e5cb29be405ce9705add2a8cd4d98671dd5427516.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325e859f-6fa0-552c-9635-1e85fe432181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6e/6e5c3047a6b369447fe080b8a3371c2b77860eb606c53f3c0d983dacabc57f31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386946} {:answer/id #uuid "02ba4a6e-7d91-59ac-97c8-c9fa06377315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/db/db231ce754b70ae329b37d1e32330c5f9fe51d5565922a0d271ad5d64721026a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386947} {:answer/id #uuid "95dab66a-fca7-50e9-9195-de730f4d199d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/60320829f744c54b2a299a2c2c7cb9665365e8a5617c9e53f1aa5dffd52ec5a2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386948} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdea625-def0-5d8c-a98c-eaa2138290f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4dc193655982bcb9b5281b20814402e85ba1aeda39769e256b74a95a47ba6db4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386949} {:answer/id #uuid "7d92a81b-eaa6-5be1-b9d4-12782c675db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9eedd0ac47d605fac550e2b7311f7406bbe1b46a733f28eb6b5b8c5460971870.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdea625-def0-5d8c-a98c-eaa2138290f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4dc193655982bcb9b5281b20814402e85ba1aeda39769e256b74a95a47ba6db4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386949} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7695c75-a86b-5bbd-981f-d33175d45aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "381c6867-01dd-581f-aef2-bf68fe1db768" :question/math-academy-id "q-119300" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/4d/4dc193655982bcb9b5281b20814402e85ba1aeda39769e256b74a95a47ba6db4.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](images/45/45a0f2e8b0c46e8b9a2985ff20c42457cd617957cd3da168a0c91171aceacfb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8676a75f-0b27-5454-9e68-fb96005d3b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/28cea5c0c95e4693ea10aedebf1f361737dff629bc961efb085c0a5f726b2d17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386953} {:answer/id #uuid "135f7456-cda5-5ee1-b9ce-ac880059fdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386954} {:answer/id #uuid "117e207d-3e92-5dbc-97ae-4930af25295b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b51e287d7e6d20932c70c6a81e565b647230416aa54e25f35546d181e4767689.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386955} {:answer/id #uuid "316da0f4-ac19-53f8-bd1f-d5b24c5db74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb596c47096ee36672f95a67be1b736c1478dce6f61ac5d7f950f922bc4a6dae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386956} {:answer/id #uuid "041cae78-e2a6-5818-975d-f875bcebd8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70a2d28e1c9e5fc7905c999d40151dc32d6cb36a129009a2d869f4f78e4cd73c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "135f7456-cda5-5ee1-b9ce-ac880059fdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386954} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0551f94-457b-5c49-a222-37da906e023c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65c21155-0ccb-52d7-9cb7-c682f8d6519d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119303" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-1?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-2),(1,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-2)}=-2-1=-3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=1-1=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a1/a161b298270d5483561400697453451cdb2ed41e4e1390fb41f63780b908d5ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e0130c-16ac-5395-beca-acc0008dbd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/92e312e02d6e90adbf1fbe65c2a77a0a52e8123955c6f21ad28577678445dbfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386960} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7d46c2-4a97-5788-bebf-1aa7b0e56068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16a230cce7b982590ddcbfba2862ca5b2df253d6796632a7c31a76cc3b42785d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386961} {:answer/id #uuid "73374ce8-196a-5a66-ab48-75923d47c23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386962} {:answer/id #uuid "0c27db64-f453-5e78-be12-77755dd285f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/727968555be54b474fcb51b972dc24c50ca7afe972d0cea5bae63f4d4db32888.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386963} {:answer/id #uuid "30c59404-76fe-5dba-8209-0ba153adf1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/0075556b1e97d2c805490deec07f0efa39257ccfc2d99117c7bcbbfc413a0fbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73374ce8-196a-5a66-ab48-75923d47c23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386962} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94ee711-e809-5096-b681-130463d852bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f1478d3-68c0-55da-a754-1a29e774dc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-119309" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1),(1,1)$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-{1}^{2}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=2(1)-{1}^{2}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=2(2)-{2}^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/21/21828e2eb3d107ea12e90d715de9a3c1350e6fd95f03b321be7ab81a8824b065.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3eb411b-62d3-53c6-80e4-868eb7539f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c7/c74a4cf84ce908132b57674ef5624e0afcd42af7c40150ac0079e25894a60ba3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386967} {:answer/id #uuid "e90af615-1ee8-5498-898e-f0df11636fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1fbb7d5a4a957b130c0835b09a6dba5c527e3b61844769b78f5517f4e4914b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386968} {:answer/id #uuid "399ebddc-b942-5c6b-8d8e-df85655665f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386969} {:answer/id #uuid "9b82c2d7-12f5-53f9-94db-a216cf94b533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/128c02e4d847e918cce92b38f4a7f09ffab6a11bf6f25ee7fd3480d6a6c3cac6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386970} {:answer/id #uuid "f54c1d72-e512-5d2c-944e-9d0b9b4f9323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/81bdcfd873677151067b3e2d542315d2f10f9b65f19a05ad3759dc5fe69a3704.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399ebddc-b942-5c6b-8d8e-df85655665f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386969} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb0f45e-2d6d-59d5-95a0-428528782598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca31a32-1c1a-5bad-882d-872d4b1f5188" :question/math-academy-id "q-119372" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{xy}?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7d/7d5df9fe81ebd1e656eefc97380ae646a1a4f64de51e4745bc31d822e022d65d.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/d0/d05d98b24783511dc6ff7518141e49d9cfefe96565f16b9d11b30d0e6fc0a5a4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84adfcaf-a598-5571-aa06-f6af588ed60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe57c0cc2d75729921eb46408e0e4a6932eba06c00034f2ec4c579c8c5627376.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386974} {:answer/id #uuid "d119e212-ad62-51f4-aa40-7ccda4c4d389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c959785ef067b41eba5e5ca5f0759acdd81c9f613c92854ba5937be6bced2c86.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386975} {:answer/id #uuid "e17b3836-865c-519c-88f1-9b2ec1f1d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac12c64fe5282c78ea926eec823cec6b66c8fad9f153c4191d39ba7751e90e25.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386976} {:answer/id #uuid "373395c0-c96b-52a6-9531-5a58b40d8884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386977} {:answer/id #uuid "48347676-3575-5e72-90f7-c8cf10b0c6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96817ea42e1d860db97d3ee6bca79e9a8b03a0fd413c87ebc5679f746da3166b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373395c0-c96b-52a6-9531-5a58b40d8884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386977} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc934043-e8c4-5447-b0e7-d94aa4cef2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1772040-8608-5c67-8243-2a903b40e108" :question/math-academy-id "q-119383" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](images/9c/9cb42cde56fcbea0c489742cab1448890728dab584395129b21794115b1e1298.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/9d/9d256bd6c44a9abc8489c360722b5c5e8243f0d4a9aa6fee03cad52e5334b385.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd4158d-6baf-5a61-96a2-74c66231d082" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a Triangular/Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbcb05c-0d49-5cbd-80eb-6b804f107249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = \\sqrt{3}$ and $λ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089274} {:answer/id #uuid "0a494e60-093c-58c4-a59c-aa2a3b60e7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no real eigenvalues" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089275} {:answer/id #uuid "323c248b-e18e-5611-b08d-9631fd900654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $λ = \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089276} {:answer/id #uuid "479da784-7457-5ebe-ab3e-66bcab6eacf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $λ =-\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089277} {:answer/id #uuid "5016ca94-d6c8-5940-9a53-df997f015259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 4$ (double root)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089278}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aed5cf4f-5cf4-5caf-81e8-f22caef29322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089273}] :question/id #uuid "b1fb4b20-223a-5224-b15e-06a76416065c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11983" :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a702ee-79d1-55c6-8b8d-dc8a7f0f77db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "336\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386981} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5df382-1586-5105-af93-061d2effa30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386982} {:answer/id #uuid "b73a068b-a624-57c6-8f23-a62443cd2759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "354\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386983} {:answer/id #uuid "13ab4dd1-997e-50aa-8938-fe73f4c8d489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "348\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386984} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5375b8-a182-53a8-881e-1b45a8a8313f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e5df382-1586-5105-af93-061d2effa30e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386982} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f65bd7f-3d3f-5045-8487-612d7385d46c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2645ff-704d-5d0e-a816-127a2f8fd501" :question/math-academy-id "q-120078" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b618c8e43dbe898a6e1c481f4f887a50bc2e8c658368876be11ec5ea269a50c.png)\n\nA company produces a chocolate bar that comes in an triangular-prism-shaped box. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" :question/worked-solution "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 36+2\\times 72+108 \\\\  & =72+144+108 \\\\  & =324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf5bf024-dbf4-5632-a63b-ad467c311d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386988} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb7051a-7e16-5762-9d1a-14890aa8ba23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386989} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcdfefe-5c84-5826-9001-dc7bb6584180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386990} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a259f4-6b6d-5a0c-b9ac-b6e251b05b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386991} {:answer/id #uuid "4b52ab78-d764-5838-8806-470f898532a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dcdfefe-5c84-5826-9001-dc7bb6584180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386990} :answer-field/id #uuid "46ad39ef-5a66-5eb0-98a2-edda8d35005f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5537c8a8-34b9-543a-94ee-574c0ca59e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120156" :question/problem "![](images/41/41260e982c1b7aa023a86ffd48e7ba0819e651a4c928adaffeeb2567b762236a.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $\\frac{2}{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6979975-c4fc-5dd6-a7c2-9f66b3cec100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d77180cde3a0c2ca1ad5187a6b64c6b7d0d282b0e673e24c50132d7e3d91ce3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386995} {:answer/id #uuid "3aebcf64-2103-5b69-9394-e1bc7f1d2130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386996} {:answer/id #uuid "00e21cf7-1e3e-5ee9-9e9d-d78979ba3a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0a6fc2c09a48ff2b3ff05cee80fe2192b3c4df73349cb9aca0ca85bb63f4b7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386997} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7a85e1-b3d8-533d-87ab-76a9deb58d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/8542e401ead2affa416f1094b01012f6cb5b1e885abb73a08c0beb81805c5f95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386998} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fdcf46-e742-5684-b33f-a377c0fd9066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/141ad1c2adb9ac4f098887a82beb041c087a717686f72aa4e2c632066775f740.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aebcf64-2103-5b69-9394-e1bc7f1d2130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386996} :answer-field/id #uuid "472a64e6-0ae4-5eb4-904b-1786b1d5396b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc3ee50e-9f02-59b6-9819-5a8625491ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120161" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=9?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=9$ is a circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473c3565-ec6d-5c40-a350-40f0312960f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387002} {:answer/id #uuid "d3843fbf-dbb3-51fd-b094-4879dedfdc5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387003} {:answer/id #uuid "3f73c315-1a02-5816-92bb-a7fa09cc60a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387004} {:answer/id #uuid "3649883e-f058-534a-86c9-ca7394593491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387005} {:answer/id #uuid "790f3473-6c69-5eb8-806a-d6f5325589db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790f3473-6c69-5eb8-806a-d6f5325589db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387006} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b530f70-0684-540c-a40c-457b7450f149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd205798-6e48-5e7d-a533-4f8c8e3d8f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-120182" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9b356319065128b1b509a35c57020db0aa18b0096be170afe0fd1738a59040c.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(-1,4)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{17}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a4ecfa-47e2-56f4-a423-468e55f93529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}h+\\frac{1}{2}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387009} {:answer/id #uuid "e23408c0-6a35-5afe-abe2-00bbdaf4622a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{4}h+\\frac{1}{4}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387010} {:answer/id #uuid "a498dbcf-0f77-508b-9c07-efe06b97d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{5}{8}h+\\frac{3}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387011} {:answer/id #uuid "748b1bc5-d773-5fa4-a285-111048597c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{8}h+\\frac{3}{4}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387012} {:answer/id #uuid "86f8cb36-0676-5c61-8d2f-37ebe1006578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f8cb36-0676-5c61-8d2f-37ebe1006578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387013} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c4f4208-0c8e-57ef-ad8c-1decee05823d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0b691a2-9da7-5f9d-b601-8f5daf61f352" :question/math-academy-id "q-120304" :question/problem "![](images/65/6560a7f05ae95638cbfe902dfa022b2d8de6b07c6e1e6f85051ccf87b92ec338.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=5:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=5:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{5}{8}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{5}{8}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b8ead8-163c-55e6-afa2-95af3681a7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102103} {:answer/id #uuid "9095931b-ff08-5a54-a2fb-955f264244c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102104} {:answer/id #uuid "955f0cc5-43f6-5a0e-af94-704b0c7a58c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102105} {:answer/id #uuid "b0555da6-a04e-5144-979c-b0a96959fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $10$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102106} {:answer/id #uuid "273e7475-d054-5f26-b9f1-7bb8aa9231b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102107}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5573e5-02b4-5a74-83f5-8f8e0b6b8adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102102}] :question/id #uuid "6ec67f6b-b339-5863-9fd2-be6322d61819" :question/math-academy-id "q-12044" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fa9802-8c91-55e9-84a5-e1363e2e7308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387016} {:answer/id #uuid "9d30e4cd-7866-5dc8-9d17-e3603b0e1d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387017} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6b1f27-9577-571f-a402-5318532c66a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} {:answer/id #uuid "6efc2e18-f131-54ef-8b65-97d65d26fb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387019} {:answer/id #uuid "4939a251-4f3d-5483-a371-05ba68aaf27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6b1f27-9577-571f-a402-5318532c66a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a-b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e3c074-2fe2-56ad-8be9-99fa6a58e9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b958283a-525c-51ab-a33e-68049ee3df0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12049" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f9e6eff196e24ddabca8d778d27e499fc1b856ef7169257a31a7cbaca8b564b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TP}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RS}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QT}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e1d5eb2f392332f69fa1507899b30b838a42ebd1c40dacddeca4edcf0f7aa8c2.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PT}=-b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8e24968141284550fac7d4e5c63333789f30ade8f7d5dd8b87fae4e012673a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT} \\\\  & =a+(-b) \\\\  & =a-b\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af1639e-fabe-50f7-9169-1b4edc031f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387023} {:answer/id #uuid "606b60d6-6925-52a7-9990-45232a53c94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387024} {:answer/id #uuid "a7eef4c8-eeed-522e-a869-4cc328c34fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387025} {:answer/id #uuid "b67293a3-a05b-5dd0-b551-121096a709c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "79\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387026} {:answer/id #uuid "d14149ef-09e5-57ac-afbe-9dbe9e2ee161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606b60d6-6925-52a7-9990-45232a53c94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387024} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f86185-7829-51c3-a1ea-36683535a8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "398ff4f2-a514-55ba-ad0a-235934ea5e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-120532" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value: \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={330}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={210}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeb3ea5c-e17a-5133-8dd6-69250aaec174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/479df1b8aafdc4c061d221cbf5079958289178d982a33bb118934850ff889373.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387030} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee7f1-310c-57d0-a498-f20e66ba5c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387031} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fa4b48-3218-5177-81c4-7d373df6d679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82bf2fb643852163329b767d2cf8767c9ed8411060092f1652143e3842727ca2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387032} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8981da-b767-535a-87cb-a0ffd8c2a5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/7931fc318d080dbace3b1fea585f5d31449fc2dd4ceda000d659cacb43096beb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387033} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe59ba9-dbba-5706-910e-8bda39e456c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/423d9cb637371b49b351145113d40905ea786029c244ac949ee5059ea5c7315b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee7f1-310c-57d0-a498-f20e66ba5c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387031} :answer-field/id #uuid "c381b20e-76c5-566c-ad47-b7731e93267b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c78c8d-b767-562b-87b5-e9b873530467" :question/math-academy-id "q-120607" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4497da57-51f2-5e5b-a855-581d16a068ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f64e9e1818e9fd9b8375e6759f120a44a04e359cd622b2c55203b4a2060716b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387037} {:answer/id #uuid "3759d07d-fa7f-5164-aa01-e27e14999cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49f77b9363357b9880779bb180ca1e7308a5e902171fed7b8d93b607691c0672.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387038} {:answer/id #uuid "77d6a0c7-a995-55e6-936b-572306b81ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4fcc340508f074840b2637e24b49f4b0a41c9558944ebccffa2da98f63ba1703.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387039} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6475a7-6342-5f40-b055-97a1c79fb242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7fc410b5c25ac19447288dbd50aa7621aac0c86f4a863787a8a12c5adb53686e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387040} {:answer/id #uuid "be8e23d6-a2dc-5c92-8f29-3d5e35067f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/845a2af0d45ea2f46e6380863a317711b33c2d6cb662a882c6b76a48efac0c79.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6475a7-6342-5f40-b055-97a1c79fb242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7fc410b5c25ac19447288dbd50aa7621aac0c86f4a863787a8a12c5adb53686e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d432361-dae3-52b3-9aaf-c1898a606d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4660e160-164d-547c-b242-5f428a9e2e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120618" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{9}=\\frac{2\\pi }{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7fc410b5c25ac19447288dbd50aa7621aac0c86f4a863787a8a12c5adb53686e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6712c057-d325-5087-b265-9e611b0283bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387044} {:answer/id #uuid "4452d378-3fea-57aa-ad62-dddc038b7c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387045} {:answer/id #uuid "c49e8952-c40a-5925-9655-9513fef89265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387046} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e4311a-dd4f-5e64-911c-b5db5bfadeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387047} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4ac064-11fb-5475-9f74-b2e7190d838c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6712c057-d325-5087-b265-9e611b0283bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387044} :answer-field/id #uuid "b941e16d-5be1-50c8-953e-51bfad0efd5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e429c53-db66-50b4-900e-8aac7266bdd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120646" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f8d434c-7a41-5c32-941b-73360744fd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90b5ba11e4ad6545e74525fe993af172a5526bb107a2d1183c257549d8fad8be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387050} {:answer/id #uuid "56119247-90eb-5a3d-805f-d15c489f0447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d92f4081a2eac0048b571b8eb1d0daa8b5cc793c708fb50830c6f20ec9b9e19f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387051} {:answer/id #uuid "91175466-067e-5c21-be1d-b89b6d1891a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c327630597027f98e30848875cc46041e6668f3c5a990d80b7fdce90f9cdc57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387052} {:answer/id #uuid "a51def5f-78b5-5278-8427-ef58fcba4528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7bfb19d7ff5dec9190b8de7b51c56814a1635a9ecae72ab39e555465bba7cf2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387053} {:answer/id #uuid "88d88ff5-fc2f-5381-b0dc-740605524b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/4679417a09b9fffe9d08f879f19dffb356fe509e694caa7280ac1be1e3b2a436.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a51def5f-78b5-5278-8427-ef58fcba4528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7bfb19d7ff5dec9190b8de7b51c56814a1635a9ecae72ab39e555465bba7cf2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387053} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d0a4565-05f3-5f19-a713-e99af8a3225d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42cb2492-ecd7-59e3-9c43-d91c9543c6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-121090" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=x(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/59/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/43/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/18/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9540b1c6-1f60-5074-bc72-614497b1d511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126027} {:answer/id #uuid "225ef1cc-e171-581b-9168-b62af522eaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126028} {:answer/id #uuid "474e66ca-38c6-57e5-867b-dc2131699afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126029} {:answer/id #uuid "4da82025-9d7b-5d93-8eaa-aa9a7a748046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126030} {:answer/id #uuid "7e15f2e9-414c-53a7-b3cf-a14270732414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126031}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8c61e26d-991b-5668-80f7-0785c37f283e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126026}] :question/id #uuid "044d4f72-bfa3-5995-a255-529e2a3f6304" :question/math-academy-id "q-121237" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y$. What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x, y)$?\n\n| $y > 0$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < 0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x < 0$ | $x > 0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd7c1dad-91bd-57f7-a2d3-c2ad5a59757b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/29/29cf79e3f216a3b33656473fd98bd04252e2abd6304fdd575ee56cd33e1f4c3d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387057} {:answer/id #uuid "e74fba45-c345-5136-9ccf-dd0ff41294b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e4fb6cb6b35a80a392e6f5091fddb7c136996927ea1810f0dd1db5d0e263996c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387058} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8aeec8-7b9a-5de3-8c61-a2066893fe39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/058a4ec5eba7a1ada3fc5b89ada73819672a19f88fbf4