| $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/3c/3cfdf7d548c430f225a78fa6c4d915d69207f0a1c090d13cdd530c90865731ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec5171c3-9b89-5b66-be82-6a7a96386a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e019652dcfef9bb06dcecb42c1544e93b2f2909cfc1a40aac1eee3cf24f73e7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387064} {:answer/id #uuid "9805a50f-0f5d-5181-bc78-cc625a280c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6cd862eaf47308e250abc6cd4016bcb84132de849b289753751840f9c44c107f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387065} {:answer/id #uuid "3936bc39-c5d9-5224-a86e-f6002674711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ea9e14e3150f7566cae93bd241c8c9c1541f0f5c447066760ae26176491fe6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387066} {:answer/id #uuid "37343435-1ffb-5cf3-987f-0dbd92e7dd64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/596e25c1d1f8a0e738d8853c17c77c2f6174a2c314a94ce1674f37b3a4c5c72b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387067} {:answer/id #uuid "09a69779-86ba-5e16-bc74-deb794663e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03b7fe2ba61cbe2608c37a78c82001f6a1fdebdb59f0be0785d55ee119ba8ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3936bc39-c5d9-5224-a86e-f6002674711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ea9e14e3150f7566cae93bd241c8c9c1541f0f5c447066760ae26176491fe6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387066} :answer-field/id #uuid "8657a476-279d-5261-9e1b-ae66167785a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "075fb7b7-04bf-5658-846e-155b9f5d2466" :question/math-academy-id "q-121261" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/c4/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/20/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{2}$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/02/02043b6cfae2cf7987b7df192143b82af474e75b7cfbfa4141a318c9aaed51e5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1459c89c-36ac-519d-a0e7-c8e2f0f9d79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/289dfd9ad3a86b2b9c7bb3f2c559e7139ee392f5e1fa4aa3313c7813ac765b34.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387071} {:answer/id #uuid "50a0800c-cf60-5973-b632-972abce091c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b64742d37a9699877701703701abed5dee1e2e71aa834abbcd1eb21c8cba6e2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387072} {:answer/id #uuid "29d0e298-dc52-583f-a5df-a086d28dbdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1d93faf299aa742b09818d806166de8ae940097d0235bda3bc037dca493ecea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387073} {:answer/id #uuid "e8933066-5f73-5b2f-926f-d2495731f8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/680d673a27ca48d3aecc84437dbabacf94b9f209bb24bec7991447da51cdcd7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387074} {:answer/id #uuid "e7746e38-d885-50e5-9768-f40101656b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/8662fe5000674a6824270b52fa8f6be5001ee67e4899930200399e9dd42e17fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8933066-5f73-5b2f-926f-d2495731f8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/680d673a27ca48d3aecc84437dbabacf94b9f209bb24bec7991447da51cdcd7a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387074} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c33503e-d9ef-51c5-9c6d-222835602310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "744c6cb7-16a3-5ccf-b812-79110a1622ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-121273" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-3)(y+1)^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/83/83fcb50be8105d19e044245edd1ad492daad4658ffe4a184ce4bd632dd78e4d4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/09/099429ef29700b1eb832aee297b3d237737260a44dd01d6fc516b07710758cce.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-3)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(2,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/15/15efb1840d7a41f4600d05dcedc0c2ea5149c85e8bc1b502d62130a7da09d1b8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "742f1bc2-d4ad-5ba5-aefd-e9a9aee172d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/9505947a6ab1ebfca0aedaeec8cde50e8859dfa7bc2b349833c9d26bd849748b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387078} {:answer/id #uuid "a74d4b2d-58cb-5696-b2f5-694615021391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8eac178458e8d2b832f7b96fa5d54fedf94be2a9574666aae48c88fe254a8629.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387079} {:answer/id #uuid "8a268c86-1f9d-59cb-9640-aafa1890e479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/870d9de880f857a1fe66851ae115575955efb12b4cd651279d7494b4cb99d022.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387080} {:answer/id #uuid "21ee3236-f1d0-512e-85a7-9eee61acbbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85074c62b73199b0dd1ad8427339c58dae3e1d4ae1df014d94415aea57f24410.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387081} {:answer/id #uuid "733a2a66-7f59-58dd-a838-3cb393b88951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea79645ad6bf13be7399d8a46d18c7b99e1f2c40d3a6c500ee3710e79fdfddb3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ee3236-f1d0-512e-85a7-9eee61acbbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85074c62b73199b0dd1ad8427339c58dae3e1d4ae1df014d94415aea57f24410.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387081} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8ebeba-ce4d-5520-ac7b-bafdeabb3251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bb23e0c-4a26-596c-aca7-3529c2ce3d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-121275" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+2)(2-y).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/38/38f5235039b0a5800651759e8da98166451ab72d25e7eaa9772c10b68c3618ff.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/a6/a68d4ca3939349cef83c3c84117df44ddacf49d587b1bc8a871918f62d9b6516.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-3,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/10/1055b7667e7b1c6d27ad7a9d28ec74ae7ed58dac262ae83b5c4028dcee3f9312.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4457c61-79cf-5b6b-9f7d-2a64aa1620cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d996ae0305472b14d52d3ec951669590aad2a249a3ce2f2643d65ad17993a899.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387085} {:answer/id #uuid "615dd0c2-a153-520e-874d-9a7ca0cdfb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec94dbaaf4cc906de62faa9c2eea1ca9028915377f18ff5bf4aa5bd8d53b2df2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc85e982-0cd2-5e9b-bb8b-963b7d7fd543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10080518e756d9d915dadc4346c23a9db90ff7d0f8df90b09f571b3f9f8aa29c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387087} {:answer/id #uuid "e3641bd9-8933-5d72-9a3e-69c90c0b9b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91ec230c1251255ad019b445ba18bc35cf46ebf5fceaac74a359ecd819e5ec1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387088} {:answer/id #uuid "1b27f675-bc47-500a-bbf3-2a7e3d600088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74cb28a25b133ac6ce3b5abcccef89620350ca901fd60ec66888ed7fee454e18.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4457c61-79cf-5b6b-9f7d-2a64aa1620cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d996ae0305472b14d52d3ec951669590aad2a249a3ce2f2643d65ad17993a899.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387085} :answer-field/id #uuid "82206e3f-a393-55e4-9c7a-d0f6c1ac5063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a662f9bf-4050-5954-8495-805c0cc66f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-121310" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{2}(y+2).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](images/26/26f786cb4b0e0d923502a6fef4c827341a5a8c5f61d497ec479742db3ee7ea87.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/0f/0f68fc744e359192c65134f941952d8cfcf928b1f74924896df09ea23a36d07f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)^{2}$ | $(y+2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,-3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](images/20/206cc195b55db8f3793f4d84d0bd3b78f21430759770c8be4c815f8db8d562fb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9e07dc2-1609-5e79-a646-1a35fc112393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387092} {:answer/id #uuid "08451189-0f9c-544a-bf3c-0ed9bc4de695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}+x+5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387093} {:answer/id #uuid "339fabab-5d9c-5f67-a828-7b2be5058246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/3}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387094} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4d99b7-abbf-597b-a573-795fd8eb1add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}-x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387095} {:answer/id #uuid "22b4d77b-f8fd-5693-8621-84c84313567a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b4d77b-f8fd-5693-8621-84c84313567a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387096} :answer-field/id #uuid "fabea5bd-4133-591e-b119-1b3b4af303a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b0cb8d7-3e11-5e09-bfb8-3963a1210c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121424" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a928b14cfe88f9aaf7b8cf5288a18f3e81e163ea65fd3a48653fcba630c11386.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=8{x}^{2}-5$ and $y=1-2x,$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/22/226ade4a3b546484ba2e9ce5f266391fb51e9fe4b62e2e78d12200ac54ecea9a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(1-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56e672f5-dce5-5fa6-adcd-1a961d81744a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}+2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387099} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba0933e-b635-5c29-8f6c-2e1fff10756f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}+x-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387100} {:answer/id #uuid "626d6993-64e2-51a1-8455-31c14edbc69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3/4}^{1}(-8{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387101} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd1781d-6f2f-5da6-8c71-74484a0b86d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-4{x}^{2}-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387102} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf3d09c-e58b-5b2f-b716-ba3fe540c25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf3d09c-e58b-5b2f-b716-ba3fe540c25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387103} :answer-field/id #uuid "337e69ec-98d4-58b7-86e9-081e8f92fe8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1495ffd4-708e-5569-a19d-b4ce8001edba" :question/math-academy-id "q-121517" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y=8{x}^{2}-4$ and $y=2-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f34492ce184169d0e720d5a2214a356236b56d6e20bd8ece584b967f074e64.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2x & =8{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4\\cdot 8\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 14}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/94/94243d27b465eca7957731385b2fe13fae36b811079d3077e2e093aa19d81ad3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-4,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(2-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-4)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dccdc3ad-6acb-57ae-83ef-95ffedfbff2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf2f96ee-1917-569d-a6f4-50cb8bf9f1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387106} {:answer/id #uuid "92423a0f-b587-53ae-bf4d-22c1e1c9e74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387107} {:answer/id #uuid "cdaed974-3e54-5911-9788-51ec7f9b0647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387108} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6be502-d592-5986-a8cc-a36f3e82278b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387109} {:answer/id #uuid "c43b0452-2260-56aa-9a6b-0ae8bc1a5e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdaed974-3e54-5911-9788-51ec7f9b0647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨-6t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387108} :answer-field/id #uuid "308b3e46-6c99-5c68-bebc-11b491287095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dff3dcdc-b2c7-599a-a4a3-788e4f4701f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-121715" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨1-3{t}^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a899207-dc76-5253-ae81-023fd5b4ab01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387113} {:answer/id #uuid "8667638f-40a4-53b0-92f9-ebcb6040b16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387114} {:answer/id #uuid "42a4d32e-7d2e-5cf2-8b72-aa67df335039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387115} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe0c24e-3f29-5b73-b12f-9c6a4612a92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387116} {:answer/id #uuid "0b47d3a6-c340-5b29-b307-970607a392ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8667638f-40a4-53b0-92f9-ebcb6040b16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387114} :answer-field/id #uuid "088a7b58-83c5-566b-8918-fa4964a4522e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04e13fd6-9b6c-5320-b070-cd6f7b64eb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-121717" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{4t+1},3t-2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4t+1}},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4(2)+1}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8036d2c2-8a2e-5c67-acd1-ce0e9d47e6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387120} {:answer/id #uuid "582500e4-c596-5232-81dc-6bc6fbee7997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387121} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd5aa7c-be5e-513f-828f-f8fb25908e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387122} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb5133-1e74-501b-977e-8689bedac6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387123} {:answer/id #uuid "362aaed2-f0c6-53a7-88e5-52f5c57042ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcd5aa7c-be5e-513f-828f-f8fb25908e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387122} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4aa5c5-7aa5-5947-9569-21ed1cfb107b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "741539b0-f482-543c-8cda-1da90dd97aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-121720" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4127db46-897b-5df1-86a3-53e7bdadfa44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387127} {:answer/id #uuid "39b826c5-137e-5e09-b3c2-5310084b404f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{1}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387128} {:answer/id #uuid "be134c04-baa4-55cf-9396-06677efdc29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387129} {:answer/id #uuid "729a5ea0-4c4c-525f-875a-f6228e2dc5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387130} {:answer/id #uuid "e79efc27-4a61-5c55-b7c6-d6c06a9a34ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{1}{3t+2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be134c04-baa4-55cf-9396-06677efdc29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387129} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ba10eb2-6642-5d01-a5e6-15242712a8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43a48947-068a-5638-90e7-fa84c34661dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-121903" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+2),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+5)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+2)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+5))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdbf7fc9-f5ad-56e7-bc07-5c375f28c1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387134} {:answer/id #uuid "cde1e1d2-30cc-5d8f-a36d-b2478fabf989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387135} {:answer/id #uuid "fe24b444-bd1d-59b6-9483-48d4cfc27308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387136} {:answer/id #uuid "114f469d-69f7-56f7-af0f-17247ed2f5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387137} {:answer/id #uuid "b6822baa-7945-54b5-ae74-9fae4b5bacf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "114f469d-69f7-56f7-af0f-17247ed2f5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387137} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe313015-93be-5870-b93a-eda3958c82aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "accdf36e-4deb-5652-9073-b1834a2b81f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121917" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}),2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(\\frac{\\pi }{8})|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(\\frac{\\pi }{8})| & =|⟨4,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c5cc12-791b-582c-8800-d09d9e5897f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{4}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387141} {:answer/id #uuid "9be7db96-b0e3-5250-88bf-48d61a4a5e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387142} {:answer/id #uuid "9d84db7f-e242-5a70-9af3-9ff09623f1f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}}{4}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387143} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbe2b31-7baa-5eb2-bdb4-0ffb641710c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387144} {:answer/id #uuid "659484fc-53c0-5be7-b82b-841304443bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9be7db96-b0e3-5250-88bf-48d61a4a5e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387142} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2a8aab-97b6-54d8-b857-404581a4b724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b79ce39-e3a6-5781-a9e4-71c93eee0b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-121920" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t-1},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t-1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨{2}^{1-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2(1)}{({1}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡2+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f37179-79e4-51bf-8ebe-89abd1aef316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387148} {:answer/id #uuid "0da758e2-24e9-5911-a1ff-649c7dcfab80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387149} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4e5413-9842-5f4e-a925-6a5e8948c4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387150} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba6730e-fe44-5356-a10c-54d122764123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387151} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1bd544-7287-5b81-8510-8426445f770b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c4e5413-9842-5f4e-a925-6a5e8948c4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387150} :answer-field/id #uuid "be03e2d7-07ae-5fa2-ad0f-25d2177c9de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19cf5879-4ba8-59d0-8ef2-68769cbabfb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121926" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1,{e}^{2t+5}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2{e}^{2t+5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4{e}^{2t+5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e29a15-4147-5867-8960-63216ce53848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-3{t}^{2},4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387155} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9cbd2e-c7c5-55e3-864c-30aa6f188461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-3{t}^{2},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387156} {:answer/id #uuid "be3cb3db-8d7a-52c1-8f40-2ad1d937dbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-6t,4t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387157} {:answer/id #uuid "27bbdf9b-4f76-567d-947f-a977bf98d1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-4t,4t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387158} {:answer/id #uuid "60c3ab87-aa2d-5bf7-abf2-3d0b53671f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-6t,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c3ab87-aa2d-5bf7-abf2-3d0b53671f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-6t,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387159} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35a47e0-7604-5013-93c7-f542f2eba76b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13d7a5d0-065f-59fe-97e0-96a304f6f591" :question/math-academy-id "q-121931" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4t-{t}^{3},2{t}^{2}+t⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{t}^{3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4-3{t}^{2},4t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-3{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2d134a-f130-5719-a37a-0473dced99f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387162} {:answer/id #uuid "d05cca3a-f678-58c9-9ae8-1f0ae55f1a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-2,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387163} {:answer/id #uuid "36910ca0-9aab-558f-8676-0ed8fe85c222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-2,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387164} {:answer/id #uuid "450d8849-1778-5e9a-be8e-29866586bb9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-8,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387165} {:answer/id #uuid "7e756be2-22c0-5ea1-958a-c6e33fbc6bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e756be2-22c0-5ea1-958a-c6e33fbc6bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-4,-18⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387166} :answer-field/id #uuid "03715ca4-0656-5fff-b954-eb1c258e1f52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "052d683a-4534-50ca-9e0f-c6b7dbb2dbeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121932" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4-2{t}^{2},1-3{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,-9{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-9{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-4,-18(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82d2cb6-1575-5905-9fba-e43762d5c85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387169} {:answer/id #uuid "d009dbe1-bfc5-5925-96e6-d5179636b515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387170} {:answer/id #uuid "8da048ba-d720-5e3b-a6d5-25d0ac604a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387171} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ca4f6-98b5-5a6e-ba81-d4fb6c5420bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387172} {:answer/id #uuid "35940c79-f66a-5ec8-864b-f082d00f5c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ca4f6-98b5-5a6e-ba81-d4fb6c5420bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387172} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e79d04-f822-521e-8366-34b8cce431a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84c7731a-fdb1-5923-b13d-b64da6ea7881" :question/math-academy-id "q-121936" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),2{t}^{4}+3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}},24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1+1)^{2}},24({1}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5bad673-14ce-5226-a6d7-5a8270a2715c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387176} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cf995e-fc5a-5173-aef4-6de220571ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387177} {:answer/id #uuid "88a13aed-bf55-51ed-850c-f35e7d195082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-1,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387178} {:answer/id #uuid "752164a6-9933-5299-951d-b40bc6f288b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387179} {:answer/id #uuid "946341a5-eeb3-5dd6-a86e-bd4baa3abc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "946341a5-eeb3-5dd6-a86e-bd4baa3abc95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387180} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc9c1e3e-bdec-57e9-a54b-161f171fa9db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7df19fa-04ae-5351-a8a0-d4ee8b09fbbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-122092" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},2{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-{2}^{3}+{C}_{1},2({2}^{2})-2+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=5$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+5,2{t}^{2}-t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1-{1}^{3}+5,2({1}^{2})-1-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493c72c7-d782-5717-bb31-6b0270646703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387182} {:answer/id #uuid "a9663772-7b34-5824-82d4-530f49fc606a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183} {:answer/id #uuid "c397de39-3c94-523e-b199-d82ded805b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387184} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22557f-8b13-5a8f-9cf2-c6c3704ac0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387185} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10713-d816-5d20-9f77-54c2d84897ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad10713-d816-5d20-9f77-54c2d84897ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387186} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7ee2bd2-6018-5d27-a3ae-041cc035912b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8f5c163-ed26-54b5-8aab-82f3a498034d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122263" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2294730-1a63-56a9-975a-ed773eb5c601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9612ef402244aad3a71fb8f8de587eb4be546ad3c1718885c1bafbb1ae1066.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387188} {:answer/id #uuid "354411d0-1865-5b34-bf67-4f56be47754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387189} {:answer/id #uuid "311b14fe-ee07-5a20-a6a5-59a701d67acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/565dab4b729e587b63826dc9f39363eaf0e941b39188f745b71a336368ed0b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387190} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a365b-59f8-5560-9234-ece8c5e30b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e863d2283ea97745102ab489bd7721ad8987c37f33b246023bb2549053f3ebf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387191} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e37acb-40d4-5fed-9de7-438853dac171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2abd84e2cd0c55cdec95ccb21c8a75270b97d6b7a3bf8b80137e38ab198c24c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354411d0-1865-5b34-bf67-4f56be47754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387189} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac480a4-ae34-526d-b6ff-99c48416aa4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5a8a53e-8c38-522a-bf60-158bb8a0f977" :question/math-academy-id "q-122407" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =2\\cdot (-2)\\,i-j=-4\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =2\\cdot (0)\\,i-j=-\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\cdot 2\\,i-j=4\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09893bf8-0fff-5ef9-996a-20b32e756f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨-12,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387195} {:answer/id #uuid "91132415-55cb-5f5d-b24b-383d4fb97852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387196} {:answer/id #uuid "03fbb036-5761-5eae-9d29-8d7f31a292e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387197} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdf6b7f-c082-5657-8bd9-1ee34337e138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387198} {:answer/id #uuid "54ed7d30-b419-5e71-bcfe-65439a4fc59c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨6,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91132415-55cb-5f5d-b24b-383d4fb97852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(1)=⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387196} :answer-field/id #uuid "31589b6c-465b-54ec-b7c0-06e23e58bc77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb4a6e90-038b-5f2c-a298-f65f1f8dbd3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122424" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨10,-1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-1,{t}^{2}-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{1},\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨10,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨3({3}^{2})-3+{C}_{1},\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-1⟩ & =⟨24+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-14$ and ${C}_{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-t-14,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨3({1}^{2})-1-14,\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-1-7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,-\\frac{23}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef9fabf9-c18c-50a1-963a-7448f08bf2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,3t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387202} {:answer/id #uuid "846cf632-7b3a-5114-8f92-b6680d7ea90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387203} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4d7265-d9e7-543f-b5ac-4cb4a42855c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387204} {:answer/id #uuid "169de5da-2a43-5074-932b-ff3949f05195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387205} {:answer/id #uuid "10fab286-e298-5bb7-b569-1f7e5bfc15e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,5t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a4d7265-d9e7-543f-b5ac-4cb4a42855c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387204} :answer-field/id #uuid "62463180-a575-5094-bd92-23503f886ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daf2173d-c92c-5783-9142-5642bf903eb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122471" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},0⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(2,3)$ and has velocity $⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},0⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t},-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{3},-t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨2,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=2$ and ${C}_{4}=4.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,4-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259822a5-969c-59bd-9409-203032b8e5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387209} {:answer/id #uuid "3abdd947-03f1-5fe3-acea-f4487dafdafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387210} {:answer/id #uuid "16948774-922d-5453-867e-9d6ae6b1b44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387211} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a7f2b7-c75d-5c1f-8bac-5e57c8a4dbd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387212} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb95f4-d026-5528-8f18-e7dc00345196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a7f2b7-c75d-5c1f-8bac-5e57c8a4dbd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387212} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3ce0b2-9f8b-54dc-a549-62477751c6e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f3b348f-3d24-534c-8714-77102243eca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122476" :question/problem "How many $5$-letter words (legitimate or made-up) can be made using the letters of the word ALGORITHMS, where each letter cannot appear in the word more than once?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ different letters in the word ALGORITHMS, so $n=10.$ We want to select $5$ of those letters to make a $5$-letter word, so we have $r=5.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different made-up words is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{5} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-5)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =30\\,240.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd8e10c-acce-5a96-8043-a1ff8e04be8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216} {:answer/id #uuid "a4067404-39d0-5695-bef0-39b4a803751d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "654cf8dd-b5e7-5cb5-b3d2-53296e067a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218} {:answer/id #uuid "8dba4b05-44bf-5ce0-9bbc-0896224934cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee24693-a37d-53cd-9c9c-68982eeb7210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4067404-39d0-5695-bef0-39b4a803751d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd1f76-c0c6-5efb-a8ef-61be55142b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c026636-3333-5149-910a-9646132297e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122540" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $(-3,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c793c767-8ecf-564e-af97-7c0e5166064b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387223} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2e31f-1ce7-5e4f-bb9e-8d3e5faf7e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387224} {:answer/id #uuid "b13d0e88-37bf-5316-8237-40c1c7c1a505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387225} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a101a7-ad66-5ad3-8847-9c8591f60fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387226} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4412e87-ff23-50d1-8bf7-efc4e936bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59160e7-9169-5e83-a5c4-ad0518caff28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/2132ac0140fb0b96a876fd04a1069d6922662c72d4d8f4b8880eacae458f8181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387230} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e66ef4-c389-503c-adac-bf6a7d3d4bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fefa684ba6cc6b2e8060c8b0860295177bade694786439317d6cee3d8cf51da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387231} {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387232} {:answer/id #uuid "e78dff93-70e1-5457-996d-4e768112af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/459b65c41ef9535d55703a64f3c9e4bfe9ffd9263a6a0f5dbf68405fb22c347d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387233} {:answer/id #uuid "0f013635-08f3-5ce8-b2d1-e6ffd2a329b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a20235e26590dbc6ddc5bdaaaa5dd484f5caf02e510bfabf2139ed7add5fa2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a97f507-5b03-5608-9bf1-7d0e5956f463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/559ffda45a088810dcaa81628b8767bfedc64ca7f6b7ebd82aebdb2ed4a7ba00.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387232} :answer-field/id #uuid "6702c2d4-7e00-59d1-8dcc-92a8c2e4e69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57d2166f-4843-548f-9e93-704a19813c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-122554" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\,j\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad47b548-a298-5cc9-8f69-70fee292db5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix That Represents a Combination of Two Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "999cf028-f43e-5387-8e1f-34f5ef402c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387236} {:answer/id #uuid "c9394ea4-8594-53a6-ab45-7e927fcf772c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387237} {:answer/id #uuid "5617da07-08b4-505f-9b56-524aea60624d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387238} {:answer/id #uuid "df9e03e4-61ec-55d1-8d42-0a576f46640f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387239} {:answer/id #uuid "ac14671c-5d80-512f-990e-8a0aa1ae7d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9394ea4-8594-53a6-ab45-7e927fcf772c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d09ee0-7f42-57fa-b70e-8a8af283baba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d0b8d1b-eb0e-577f-861b-e28e4941bce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-12257" :question/problem "Construct the matrix of the linear transformation that acts on a vector in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it rotates the vector by $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin, and then\n\n- stretches the result by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{6}$ parallel to the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation and let $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretch. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Q & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $QP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QP & =(\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}])(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f90d0fcc-df3f-54ef-aac5-4852b0a9e794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-6{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387243} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6acbd4-d635-575c-943b-644683f18c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387244} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bd4d7e-4781-5218-931d-c97cde2ff693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387245} {:answer/id #uuid "cddcdbf9-66b3-51d5-90ba-30cc5ce21442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387246} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfcb486-31a0-5c12-b0ed-93d029be6c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcfcb486-31a0-5c12-b0ed-93d029be6c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387247} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac816d5f-c719-59f3-837c-14f7205ae0a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b50e6e3-88d1-56d2-a993-e2318e36e7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122582" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(6,0)$ to the point $(0,6)$ in the first quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $6$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/c1/c1e3332017b795ed6733a0547e6e449fdba3874f1d1e755118a0687ccaf7448c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
